# V. Six National Conservation Capabilities

A mature American conservation system requires six capabilities.

## 1. Ecological Intelligence

The ability to know what is happening.

Includes:

* monitoring;
* science;
* data;
* modeling;
* remote sensing;
* traditional and Indigenous knowledge where appropriately governed;
* citizen science;
* forecasting;
* early-warning systems.

## 2. Cross-Jurisdiction Coordination

The ability to act when ecological systems cross institutional boundaries.

## 3. Institutional Capacity

The people, expertise, administrative systems, authority, and organizational memory required to implement conservation.

## 4. Conservation Finance

The ability to provide appropriate resources at the appropriate scale and timescale.

## 5. Performance Management

The ability to determine whether conservation interventions actually produce intended results.

## 6. Long-Term Stewardship

The ability to maintain ecological and institutional gains across decades.

