# X. The Conservation Evidence Hierarchy

Conservation decisions should distinguish different levels of evidence.

### Level 1 — Observation

Something changed.

### Level 2 — Association

The change coincided with an intervention.

### Level 3 — Mechanistic Evidence

There is a credible mechanism connecting intervention and outcome.

### Level 4 — Comparative Evidence

Alternative explanations have been evaluated through appropriate comparison.

### Level 5 — Strong Causal Evidence

Evidence provides substantial confidence that the intervention caused the observed effect.

Not every conservation decision can reach Level 5.

The objective is not to impose an impossible evidentiary standard.

It is to make the strength of evidence visible.

